1: # The actual global rho family and its loop groupoid
2: 
3: Working proof following K49 and K53. It retains two source-zero fan
4: objects and an unevaluated infinity return. No numerical source-zero
5: class is assigned.
6: 
7: Fix one of the two E7 one-cap data:
8: \[
9:  (a,b,m)=(1,2,4p)\quad\hbox{or}\quad(2,1,4p-1),
10:  \qquad n=\pm1,\quad N=km,\quad k>0.
11: \]
12: The marked scalar is \(\lambda(v)=v^N\lambda_0\). Put
13: \[
14:  v=\rho^2-1,\qquad
15:  E_v:\ Y^2=X^3+(1-2v)X^2+TX+v^2,\qquad G=(0,v),
16:  \quad T_0=v^2-2v.
17: \]
18: 
19: ## RG.1. A bounded parameter surface and its actual charts
20: 
21: Take a large finite \(R>2\), and remove small disjoint discs around
22: \(\rho=-1,0,1\) from the disc \(|\rho|\le R\). Denote this compact
23: four-holed surface by \(B_R\). Its two points over \(v=1/8\) are
24: filled using the actual family of K53. We choose \(\lambda_0\) in
25: the common contracting range on this compact parameter surface.
26: This does not presume that the same fixed scalar remains
27: contracting throughout a punctured neighbourhood of infinity.
28: 
29: All source equations and the canonical Cartier divisors are the
30: ones displayed above. The discriminant of their cubic in \(X\),
31: viewed as a polynomial in \(T\), has parameter discriminant
32: \[
33:  16v^2(v+1)^6(1-8v)^3.
34: \]
35: Thus on this bounded parameter surface the only excluded source
36: degenerations are the two source-zero lifts and the selected
37: collision. The unselected type-II point is precisely the disc
38: already constructed in K53.
39: 
40: Choose a single cap-overlap annulus sufficiently far out in the
41: \(T\)-plane to avoid all finite critical values for the compact
42: range of \(v\). The III-star root equations are
43: \[
44:  y'^2=x'^3+(1-2v)z^{2p}x'^2+
45:  \begin{cases}x',&m=4p,\\zx',&m=4p-1,\end{cases}
46:  +v^2z^{6p},\qquad G'=(0,vz^{3p}).
47: \]
48: Their central models, finite kernel branch and root-line
49: corrections are the ones in K49 and K53. They are uniform for
50: bounded \(v\) after shrinking the root disc. The norm-ratio has
51: the actual nonzero central value \(-2\) or \(+2\), respectively.
52: Consequently its logarithm and chosen root extend on the root
53: disc times the bounded \(v\)-disc with that reference value.
54: The same finite averaging preserves the literal root equation
55: \(A_0^m=B_0^n\) and every finite character. No cube root of \(v\)
56: is used to define these global cap charts.
57: 
58: The full reference annular map \(F_0\) of K49 is continued on
59: this fixed cap annulus. Its regular elliptic source, root,
60: Cartier and norm maps are holomorphic for every \(v\) in that
61: bounded disc, including zero. The parameter-disc exponential
62: lifting and the fixed-contour Cauchy splitting of RCZ.5 therefore
63: apply on the entire bounded disc, with the same full reference
64: map. One may choose the fixed cap annulus this large at the
65: initial reference; transport to it is through the literal source
66: and cap charts, carrying every integral annular period. The
67: construction retains the entire reference \(d\delta\) and the
68: reduced doubled period. Its restriction near \(v=0\) is the
69: marked continuation used in K49.
70: 
71: The selected cusp has the exact normalization
72: \[
73:  X=s^2-1,\qquad Y=s(s^2-\rho^2),\qquad
74:  Z=\frac{s-\rho}{s+\rho}.
75: \]
76: The marked point \(G\) is \(s=-1\), so
77: \[
78:  P_G=\frac{1+\rho}{1-\rho}.
79: \]
80: It is a nonzero finite function on \(B_R\). The unique small
81: Tate period and the physical height \(q_1=T-T_0\) satisfy
82: \[
83:  \left.\frac{Q}{q_1}\right|_{q_1=0}
84:        =-\frac{v}{64(v+1)^3}
85:        =-\frac{\rho^2-1}{64\rho^6}.                 \tag{RG.1}
86: \]
87: The actual unit \(Q/q_1\), not just its leading coefficient, is
88: retained in the charts. The chosen fourth root of the nodal
89: \(c_4\) is \(2\rho\). Hence the Tate source coordinate is fixed
90: over the rho surface; there is no unrecorded branch interchange.
91: The lift of \(G\) is fixed by its displayed finite nodal limit.
92: Any alternative continuation differing by \(Q^j\) would have
93: limit zero or infinity for \(j\ne0\), so that integral ambiguity
94: does not occur. The theta/Cartier line map then fixes the line
95: coordinate as well.
96: 
97: A chosen periodic cusp fan \(\mathcal T\) therefore gives actual
98: toric charts over this entire bounded parameter surface.
99: Nonzero unit windings in (RG.1) and \(P_G\) remain in their
100: transition maps; no logarithm is used to discard them.
101: The uniform norm shells, finite caps and compact-base gluing
102: give a smooth proper sphere family over \(B_R\), with the
103: prescribed physical circle. The cap and selected-cusp charts
104: just specified identify the actual endpoint groupoid below.
105: 
106: ## RG.2. The full scalar cut and its forward endpoint arrows
107: 
108: Choose \(t=\log v/(2\pi i)\) on parameter cuts and
109: \[
110:  s_\infty=\frac{\log q_\infty}{2\pi i},\qquad
111:  h=1-T_0(v)q_\infty,\qquad q_1=q_\infty^{-1}h.
112: \]
113: The actual ordinary-to-cap cochain is
114: \[
115:  F_t=F_0+nk\,t\,s_\infty\delta.
116: \]
117: For an integer change \(t\mapsto t+w\), its forward arrows are
118: \[
119:  R^{\rm ord}_w=q_1^{nkw},\qquad
120:  R^{\rm cap}_w=h^{nkw}.                              \tag{RG.2}
121: \]
122: Multiplication here denotes the actual line maps. Their square is
123: \[
124:  F_{t+w}\circ R^{\rm ord}_w
125:         =R^{\rm cap}_w\circ F_t,                     \tag{RG.3}
126: \]
127: because the additional factor in \(F\) is \(q_\infty^{nkw}\).
128: This retains the direction: \(R_w\) is the map from the initial
129: lifted fibre to the terminal lifted fibre. The physical forward
130: return uses its inverse as the closing arrow.
131: 
132: These are maps of every retained local model. On the cap, \(h\)
133: is an invariant nonzero unit, so its multiplication commutes
134: with the root, source, line and norm maps. At the selected cusp,
135: \(q_1^{nkw}\) is its integral line-height translation. Reindexing
136: by the corresponding suspension period leaves a bounded toric
137: multiplier, with its full source and line factors. Thus it
138: extends through the periodic fan as the actual integral
139: reindexing. Its inverse extends too. The finite characters and
140: the total cyclic lattice cocycle stay in \(A_0^m=B_0^n\).
141: 
142: The scalar-root parameter itself is single valued:
143: \[
144:  r=v^k r_0,\qquad r_0^m=\lambda_0,\qquad
145:  A=r^nA_0,\quad B=r^mB_0.
146: \]
147: The cut in (RG.2) is consequently the retained annular gluing
148: cut, not a newly inserted root character. The arrows obey
149: \(R_{w+w'}=R_{w'}R_w\) literally. For the positive local rho
150: loops the integers are
151: \[
152:  \begin{array}{c|rrrr}
153:  \text{puncture}&-1&0&1&\infty\\ \hline
154:  \operatorname{wind}v&1&0&1&-2\\
155:  \operatorname{wind}r&k&0&k&-2k .
156:  \end{array}                                           \tag{RG.4}
157: \]
158: The infinity orientation in this table is the positive
159: \(\epsilon=1/\rho\) orientation.
160: 
161: ### RG.2a. The actual source-zero closing on every cusp chart
162: 
163: Near the lift \(\rho=1\), retain the two-node Tate/Cartier
164: coordinates of SZ.39--44. With \(q=q_1\), their periods are
165: \[
166:  h:(Z,V)\mapsto(\kappa qZ,\alpha V),\qquad
167:  g_{\rm per}:(Z,V)\mapsto(gZ,\eta qV),
168: \]
169: where
170: \[
171:  \kappa=q_2u_Q,\quad \alpha=P^a,\quad
172:  g=P^{-b},\quad
173:  \eta=2(-1)^{b+1}\lambda_0v^Nc_X^{-2},\quad
174:  P=q_2^{-1}u_P,\quad c_X=q_2u_X.
175:                                                         \tag{RG.4a}
176: \]
177: All the displayed \(u\)'s are the actual nonzero two-node
178: units with their extending logarithms. At \(q_1=0\), the
179: function \(q_2\) has a simple zero in \(v\). A positive \(v\)
180: loop therefore has the complete period-unit winding matrix
181: \[
182:  \begin{pmatrix}
183:   \operatorname{wind}\kappa&\operatorname{wind}g\\
184:   \operatorname{wind}\alpha&\operatorname{wind}\eta
185:  \end{pmatrix}
186:  =
187:  \begin{pmatrix}1&b\\-a&N-2\end{pmatrix}.              \tag{RG.4b}
188: \]
189: Its trace is \(N-1\). In particular the three raw normal
190: determinants in the labelled six-chart plumbing have that
191: winding; for even \(N\) their underlying real normal loops
192: are nontrivial in \(\pi_1(SO(4))\). The fixed-labelled-vertex
193: ordinary product used for the selected rho loop is not used
194: in this source-zero comparison.
195: 
196: The endpoint itself is also retained. Put \(L=nk\). The
197: forward arrow of (RG.2) at this puncture is
198: \[
199:              R_{\rm ord}:(Z,V,q)\mapsto(Z,q^LV,q).
200:                                                         \tag{RG.4c}
201: \]
202: For the two triangle charts
203: \[
204:  T^+_{i,j}:\quad q=x_0x_1x_2,\quad Z=q^ix_1,\quad V=q^jx_2,
205: \qquad
206:  T^-_{i,j}:\quad q=y_0y_1y_2,\quad
207:  Z=q^iy_0y_1,\quad V=q^jy_0y_2,
208: \]
209: this sends the index \((i,j)\) to \((i,j+L)\), with the
210: three chart coordinates unchanged. To return to the chosen
211: index representatives apply the actual period
212: \(g_{\rm per}^{-L}\). On their dense tori the resulting map is
213: \[
214:              (Z,V)\mapsto(g^{-L}Z,\eta^{-L}V).        \tag{RG.4d}
215: \]
216: Consequently its literal maps in the named charts are
217: \[
218:  \begin{split}
219:  T^+:\ &(x_0,x_1,x_2)\mapsto
220:        ((g\eta)^Lx_0,g^{-L}x_1,\eta^{-L}x_2),\\
221:  T^-:\ &(y_0,y_1,y_2)\mapsto
222:        ((g\eta)^{-L}y_0,\eta^Ly_1,g^Ly_2).
223:  \end{split}                                         \tag{RG.4e}
224: \]
225: These formulas retain the complete bounded multipliers at the
226: endpoint, rather than replacing the endpoint by identity in
227: the six named charts. Every exponent is integral. They glue
228: by the actual period maps and extend to the toric boundaries.
229: For another periodic fan the same proof uses its character
230: matrix: (RG.4c) translates every height-one vertex by
231: \((0,L)\), and (RG.4d) is the remaining bounded torus map.
232: The physical return uses the inverse of (RG.4e) after path
233: transport. The cap endpoint is the unchanged-index map
234: \[
235:        (X_j,Y_j)\mapsto(h^LX_j,h^{-L}Y_j)
236: \]
237: with its actual finite root action retained. Equations
238: (RG.3) and (RG.4c--e) give their full common overlap.
239: 
240: The opposite source-zero lift is obtained by the literal
241: reflection in RG.3; its fan is \(J\mathcal T\), and its
242: closing is the conjugate of these full maps. No identity
243: closing, labelled product, or omitted chain reindexing is
244: assumed in (RG.8).
245: 
246: ## RG.3. Reflection as an actual arrow between the two fan objects
247: 
248: At \(-\rho\) the source and cap data depend on the same \(v\).
249: The selected normalization exchanges its two branches. The
250: literal Tate and line map is
251: \[
252:  \mathcal R_\rho:\ (Z,V)\longmapsto(Q/Z,V),
253:  \qquad P\longmapsto P^{-1}.                          \tag{RG.5}
254: \]
255: It is the identity on the underlying marked source and its
256: Cartier divisor, expressed in the two actual Tate coordinate
257: systems. The line arrow follows from the theta identities,
258: without a suppressed multiplier:
259: \[
260:  \frac{\Theta_Q(QP/Z)}{\Theta_Q(Q/Z)}
261:        =\frac{\Theta_Q(Z/P)}{\Theta_Q(Z)}.
262: \]
263: For \(M_a\), apply the same identity to \(P^a\), retaining its
264: actual Cartier representative. The ordinary source, cap and
265: \(F_0\) maps are carried identically because they depend on
266: \(v\). Equation (RG.3) is also intertwined, since \(q_1,h\) are
267: unchanged and the line coordinate in (RG.5) is unchanged.
268: 
269: The fan map in the original lattice is
270: \[
271:  (x_Z,x_V,h)\longmapsto(-x_Z+h,x_V,h).
272: \]
273: Its linear two-dimensional part is
274: \(J=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1)\); the integral height translation
275: and the nonzero unit \(Q/q_1\) are retained. This gives the
276: actual arrow
277: \[
278:  \mathcal X_{\mathcal T,\rho}\longrightarrow
279:              \mathcal X_{J\mathcal T,-\rho}.
280: \]
281: Its square is the identity on all charts, including the cap.
282: It preserves the physical circle and the complex orientation.
283: The parameter map \(\rho\mapsto-\rho\) has positive local
284: complex degree one at both source-zero lifts.
285: 
286: Let \(Z_{\mathcal T}\) denote the positive return near
287: \(\rho=1\), with its actual full marked data, and let
288: \(Z^-_{\mathcal T}\) denote that near \(\rho=-1\). Then the
289: actual family arrow (RG.5) proves
290: \[
291:                   [Z^-_{\mathcal T}]=[Z_{J\mathcal T}]. \tag{RG.6}
292: \]
293: This does not identify \(Z_{\mathcal T}\) with
294: \(Z_{J\mathcal T}\). The two fan objects remain distinct inputs
295: to the loop relation.
296: 
297: ## RG.4. The cut-surface groupoid relation and the selected loop
298: 
299: Choose a rectangular outer boundary containing the three holes,
300: with disjoint vertical slits from their lower points to its
301: lower edge. Use its lower-right point as basepoint. Walking the
302: boundary of the cut-open disc, and then reversing that boundary
303: word, gives based positive meridians in the order
304: \[
305:              \gamma_+\gamma_0\gamma_-\gamma_\infty=1. \tag{RG.7}
306: \]
307: Concatenation in this equation means traversal from left to
308: right. The outer positive infinity meridian is clockwise in
309: the rho plane. The slit paths give actual transport maps from
310: the reference fibre to the four local fibres.
311: 
312: Choose a smooth connection for the actual family over the
313: cut surface, averaged over the physical circle. On a lifted
314: parameter path, let \(P_\gamma\) be its transport. Its endpoint
315: in the reference fibre is
316: \[
317:              \Phi_\gamma=R_{w(\gamma)}^{-1}P_\gamma,
318: \]
319: with (RG.2), and with the slit-path conjugations included.
320: The total integer in (RG.7) is \(1+0+1-2=0\), so its total
321: closing arrow is literally \(R_0=1\). A contraction of the
322: cut-disc boundary gives, by lifting its paths through this
323: connection, an isotopy of the product of its four actual
324: physical returns to the identity. Thus, as arrows on the
325: reference oriented sphere,
326: \[
327:        \Phi_\infty\Phi_-\Phi_0\Phi_+\simeq\operatorname{Id}.
328:                                                                \tag{RG.8}
329: \]
330: No fibrewise conjugacy alone is used to deduce this relation.
331: It comes from the actual family on the cut surface and its
332: literal endpoint maps.
333: 
334: Near \(\rho=0\), take the analytic cube root \(u^3=v\) with
335: \(u(0)^3=-1\). In the \(G=(0,1)\) source coordinates put
336: \[
337:  \xi=x-u,\qquad \sigma=\rho/u.
338: \]
339: The selected curve is exactly
340: \[
341:  y^2=\xi^2(\xi+\sigma^2),\qquad
342:  q=\frac{y^2-\xi^2(\xi+\sigma^2)}{\xi+u}.
343: \]
344: These are the actual F56 source coordinates. The parameter
345: \(\sigma=\rho/u\) has nonzero complex derivative \(u(0)^{-1}\).
346: The normalized source point has
347: \(s_{\rm norm}=s/u\), so its Tate coordinate is exactly
348: \((s-\rho)/(s+\rho)\), with no further reflection. The
349: Cartier and root maps are the analytic local coordinate maps
350: of K49/F51; their parameter units have extending logarithms.
351: The scalar ratio
352: \(\lambda(v)/\lambda(-1)\) has an extending logarithm on this
353: disc. Its explicit homotopy through that logarithm, with the
354: same root/line cochains, identifies the positive local return
355: with F56's fixed-scalar return. For the initial fan and its
356: reflected fan, K45/F56 therefore give
357: \[
358:                          [\Phi_0]=1.
359: \]
360: The two unselected points over \(v=1/8\) have the actual
361: disc extensions of K53 and give zero returns.
362: 
363: Write \(I_{\mathcal T}\) for the positive infinity return in
364: this actual bounded-parameter family. Applying the oriented
365: mapping-class identification with \(\Theta_7\), and using the
366: actual slit-path conjugations in (RG.8), gives
367: \[
368:  [Z_{\mathcal T}]+[Z_{J\mathcal T}]+1+[I_{\mathcal T}]
369:                   =0\quad\hbox{in }\Theta_7.          \tag{RG.9}
370: \]
371: Conjugation disappears here because this oriented group is
372: abelian; the underlying family arrows were retained before
373: passing to it. K49 separately identifies each original
374: positive \(u\)-return with three positive \(v\)-returns.
375: No value or division is assigned to either unknown term.
376: 
377: ## RG.5. The infinity scalar path and the independent scalar parameter
378: 
379: The term \(I_{\mathcal T}\) is defined by the actual positive
380: \(\epsilon=1/\rho\) loop, before any change to the infinity
381: source coordinates. Its literal scalar is
382: \[
383:  \lambda=(\epsilon^{-2}-1)^N\lambda_0,\qquad
384:  r=\epsilon^{-2k}(1-\epsilon^2)^k r_0.                \tag{RG.10}
385: \]
386: The unit \((1-\epsilon^2)^k\) has an extending logarithm;
387: using that logarithm gives an actual based homotopy to a
388: root-scalar loop of winding \(-2k\). At a fixed sufficiently
389: small epsilon circle, all amplitudes are chosen in a common
390: contracting range.
391: 
392: For clarity, one can use the actual source construction with
393: independent root-scalar variable \(r\). Its maps are
394: \[
395:  B=r^mB_0,\qquad A=r^nA_0,\qquad
396:  F=F_0+n\frac{\log r}{2\pi i}s_\infty\delta.
397: \]
398: The positive \(r\)-loop has the full forward arrows
399: \(q_1^n\) and \(h^n\). These commute with the retained source
400: charts and finite groupoid maps, so this is an actual family
401: over the epsilon annulus times a small \(r\)-annulus. The
402: parameter square \((\epsilon,r)=(\epsilon_*e^{2\pi is},
403: r_*e^{2\pi i(ps+qt)})\) gives the usual product-parameter
404: homotopies with all those endpoint arrows retained.
405: 
406: Denote by \(I_{\mathcal T}(j)\) the actual coupled loop of this
407: family with one positive epsilon turn and \(r\)-winding \(j\).
408: Then
409: \[
410:  I_{\mathcal T}(j)-I_{\mathcal T}(j')
411:        =(j-j')A^{\rm scalar}_{\mathcal T}.             \tag{RG.11}
412: \]
413: This is a comparison of actual loops in the same constructed
414: two-parameter family. In particular \(I_{\mathcal T}\) in
415: (RG.9) is \(I_{\mathcal T}(-2k)\). A different local rescaling
416: \(\lambda=\epsilon^{N_\infty}\lambda_\infty\), with
417: \(N_\infty=k_\infty m\), is the distinct loop
418: \(I_{\mathcal T}(k_\infty)\), with difference
419: \[
420:  I_{\mathcal T}(-2k)-I_{\mathcal T}(k_\infty)
421:        =-(2k+k_\infty)A^{\rm scalar}_{\mathcal T}.
422: \]
423: An identification of the latter loop with another infinity
424: model must still carry that model's complete source, root,
425: Cartier, annular and normal arrows. No such identification or
426: infinity numerical return is asserted by this note.
427: 
428: For the initial and reflected vertical-edge fans the actual
429: fixed-source scalar comparison of K25/K26 gives
430: \[
431:  A^{\rm scalar}=0\quad(a,b,m)=(1,2,4p),\qquad
432:  A^{\rm scalar}=1-3p\quad(a,b,m)=(2,1,4p-1).
433: \]
434: This scalar input does not identify the two source-zero fan
435: returns in (RG.9).
